.\" Manpage for whoisd (One Ring multi-RIR WHOIS server).
.\" Render with:    man ./whoisd.8
.\" Lint with:      mandoc -Tlint whoisd.8
.\" Install:        install -m644 whoisd.8 /usr/share/man/man8/
.TH WHOISD 8 "2026-06-28" "One Ring 1.0" "System Administration Commands"
.\" Update date and version together; the HTML translation at
.\" docs/whoisd.8.html should be kept in sync with this file by hand.

.SH NAME
whoisd \- One Ring multi-RIR WHOIS server

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whoisd
.RB [ \-c
.IR file " | " \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-s " | " \-\-stats ]
.RB [ \-r " | " \-\-reload ]
.RB [ \-v " | " \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-h " | " \-\-help ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whoisd
is a multi-core WHOIS server (RFC 3912) that downloads, merges, and serves
the bulk RPSL dumps of all five Regional Internet Registries (RIPE, ARIN,
APNIC, LACNIC, AFRINIC) from a single TCP endpoint. One query reaches every
registry regardless of which one owns the address space.
.PP
The server keeps the merged database in memory, indexed by primary key,
NIC handle, domain name, and a 128-bit binary radix trie of IP prefixes
(longest-prefix-match returns the most specific allocation). Each upstream
is polled on a configurable cadence with conditional
.B If-Modified-Since
requests, and the in-memory store is rebuilt only when at least one source
actually changed. A UTC time-of-day anchor guarantees at least one fresh
attempt around the RIRs' daily publish window.
.PP
A live stats subsystem tracks every in-flight connection (source, query,
state, duration, bytes transferred) and dumps a JSON snapshot to disk on a
configurable cadence (sub-second supported). An optional embedded HTTP
listener serves both a single-file dashboard and the JSON for external
consumers.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fIfile\fR,  " " \fB\-\-config\fR " " \fIfile\fR
Path to the YAML configuration file. Default:
.IR /etc/whoisd.yaml .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stats
Fetch the live status JSON from the running server's stats HTTP listener
(as configured by
.BR stats.http_bind )
and print a pretty, multi-column summary to standard output. The endpoint
URL is discovered from the same configuration file. Exits without starting
the server.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reload
Signal the running
.B whoisd
to reload its configuration. Looks up the pid file (see
.BR FILES )
and sends
.BR SIGHUP .
Exits 0 on a successful signal delivery, 1 if no pid file is found or the
signal cannot be delivered. Does not wait for the reload to complete on
the daemon side — observe the result via the daemon's log output or
.IR "whoisd --stats" .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the binary's version string and exit. The string uses a two-number
.RI ( MAJOR.MINOR )
scheme and can be stamped at build time via
.IR "go build -ldflags \(dq-X main.Version=1.2\(dq" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the built-in usage summary and exit.

.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
The configuration file is YAML. All fields have sensible defaults; an empty
file is a valid configuration but you must enable at least one source under
.BR dbase.sources .
The top-level keys are
.BR server ,
.BR dbase ,
.BR acl ,
.BR rate_limit ,
.BR stats ,
and
.BR log .

.SS server
.TP
.B bind
TCP listen address for the WHOIS protocol, in
.B host:port
form. Default:
.BR 0.0.0.0:43 .
Binding to port 43 requires either root or
.IR "setcap cap_net_bind_service=+ep ./whoisd" .
.TP
.B read_timeout
Maximum time to wait for the client's query line. Default:
.BR 5s .
.TP
.B write_timeout
Maximum time spent writing the response. Default:
.BR 30s .
.TP
.B max_concurrent
Upper bound on concurrent in-flight queries. Excess clients receive
.IR "%% server busy, try again" .
Default:
.BR 1024 .
.TP
.B max_query_bytes
Caps the query line length to defeat abusive clients. Default:
.BR 512 .
.TP
.B pid_file
Path the daemon writes its pid to on startup, and that
.B whoisd \-\-reload
reads to locate the running instance. The parent directory is created
(0755) if missing. Set to the empty string to disable pid-file management
entirely (e.g. when running under a supervisor that owns the pid via other
means);
.B \-\-reload
then has nothing to signal. Default:
.IR /run/whoisd.pid .

.SS dbase
.TP
.B cache_dir
Local directory where downloaded
.IR *.gz
RIR dumps are cached. One subdirectory per source. Default:
.IR ./var/dbase .
.TP
.B poll_interval
How often to ask each upstream "has anything changed?". Polls are cheap
because of conditional GETs. Default:
.BR 1h .
Minimum allowed:
.BR 30s .
.TP
.B daily_at
UTC time-of-day
.RB ( HH:MM )
at which to schedule one guaranteed refresh attempt per day. Picks up the
RIRs' daily publishes promptly even if
.B poll_interval
is long. Set to the empty string
.RB ( """" )
to disable the anchor. Default:
.BR 02:00 .
.TP
.B retry_on_failure
Backoff applied between attempts after a refresh fails. Default:
.BR 5m .
.TP
.B sources
List of upstream databases to fetch and merge. Each entry is an object with
the following fields:
.RS
.TP
.B name
Symbolic name used as the per-source cache subdirectory and in stats
output (e.g.
.BR ripe ,
.BR arin ).
.TP
.B enabled
Whether to fetch this source. Setting any source to
.B false
skips it.
.TP
.B format
.BR split " | " single .
.B split
fetches one
.B .gz
per RPSL class from
.BR ${url}/${db_name}.db.${class}.gz
(RIPE, APNIC).
.B single
fetches one large
.B .gz
directly from
.B url
(ARIN, LACNIC, AFRINIC).
.TP
.B url
Directory URL for
.BR split ,
or the full file URL for
.BR single .
.TP
.B db_name
File-name prefix used by the
.B split
format. Defaults to
.B name
if omitted.
.TP
.B classes
List of RPSL classes to fetch (only meaningful for
.BR split ).
Typical full set: inetnum, inet6num, route, route6, aut-num, as-block,
as-set, domain, mntner, organisation, person, role, irt, key-cert,
filter-set, inet-rtr, peering-set, route-set, rtr-set.
.RE

.SS acl
The ACL is a Binary Encoded Radix Tree (BERT) of CIDR prefixes covering
both IPv4 and IPv6 in one structure. The longest matching prefix wins.
.TP
.B default
Policy applied when no explicit allow/deny rule matches:
.BR allow " | " deny .
Default:
.BR allow .
.TP
.B allow
List of CIDRs (or bare IPs interpreted as
.BR /32 / /128 )
that are permitted. Crucially, sources matched by an entry in
.B allow
.B are also exempt from the rate limiter
(see
.BR rate_limit ).
Sources permitted only via the default policy are still rate-limited.
.TP
.B deny
List of CIDRs that are explicitly rejected with
.IR "%% access denied by policy" .
.PP
The loopback prefixes
.BR 127.0.0.0/8
and
.B ::1/128
are always implicitly allowed (and rate-limit-exempt) so operators can
always reach the server locally.

.SS rate_limit
Per-source token-bucket limiter. Source addresses are aggregated to a
configurable prefix length to prevent scanners from hiding in address
space.
.TP
.B enabled
Master switch. Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.B rps
Sustained queries per second per aggregated source. Required when
.B enabled
is true.
.TP
.B burst
Token-bucket size above
.B rps
before throttling kicks in. Required when
.B enabled
is true.
.TP
.B v4_prefix
Prefix length to aggregate IPv4 sources by (0\(en32). Default:
.BR 32
(per-IP) — most operators want
.BR 24
to lump abusive subnets together.
.TP
.B v6_prefix
Prefix length to aggregate IPv6 sources by (0\(en128). Default:
.BR 128 .
Typical:
.BR 48 .
.TP
.B idle_ttl
How long an idle per-source bucket lives in memory before garbage
collection. Default:
.BR 10m .

.SS stats
.TP
.B enabled
Master switch for the stats subsystem (counters, live connection registry,
JSON dumps, optional HTTP listener). Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.B dump_path
Path of the JSON file rewritten atomically every
.BR dump_interval .
Empty disables the disk dump. The HTTP listener can still serve in-memory
snapshots even with no disk dump configured.
.TP
.B dump_interval
Cadence at which the snapshot is regenerated and (optionally) flushed to
disk. Sub-second values are allowed
.RB ( """200ms""" ,
.BR """500ms""" ).
Minimum:
.BR 50ms .
Default:
.BR 1s .
.TP
.B http_bind
TCP listen address for the embedded HTTP dashboard. Serves
.B GET /
(single-file HTML dashboard) and
.B GET /stats.json
(current snapshot). Empty disables the listener.

.SS log
.TP
.B level
Verbosity. One of
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.BR warn ,
.BR error ,
or
.B none
(also accepts
.B off
or
.BR silent ).
Each level includes everything above it. Default:
.BR info .
.PP
A small set of lifecycle events (server starting with version, each
listener bind, each (re)load completion) is logged at a custom
.B NOTICE
level above
.BR error ,
so it is emitted regardless of the configured filter except
.BR none .

.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read the configuration file and apply the
.B reloadable subset:
.BR acl.* ,
.BR rate_limit.* ,
.BR log.level ,
.BR stats.dump_interval ,
and
.BR server.bind .
Each sub-step is independent — a failure in one is logged and skipped, the
others still apply. Changes to fields outside this subset (notably
.BR dbase.* ,
the
.B server
deadlines, and
.BR server.max_concurrent )
require a full restart. The same effect is available from the CLI via
.BR "whoisd \-\-reload" .
.TP
.B SIGINT, SIGTERM
Initiate a graceful shutdown: stops accepting new connections, waits for
in-flight queries to finish, flushes the final stats snapshot, and exits.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/whoisd.yaml
Default configuration file location (override with
.BR \-c ).
.TP
.IR cache_dir /\fIsource\fR/\fIsource\fR.db.\fIclass\fR.gz
Cached RIR dumps. Each enabled source has its own subdirectory; the file
naming mirrors the upstream layout (or just
.IR source.db.gz
for single-file formats).
.TP
.I dump_path
The current stats JSON snapshot, atomically rewritten every
.BR dump_interval .
.TP
.I server.pid_file
Configurable pid file path. Written on startup and read back by
.BR "whoisd \-\-reload" .
Default:
.IR /run/whoisd.pid .

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown after SIGINT/SIGTERM.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure: initial database load failed, the listener died, or the
server otherwise stopped on an error.
.TP
.B 2
Startup failure: configuration could not be loaded, parsed, validated, or
the ACL could not be built.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run the server with a custom config:
.PP
.RS
.nf
whoisd \-\-config /etc/whoisd.yaml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Query the running server (default WHOIS client, port from
.BR server.bind ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
whois \-h 127.0.0.1 \-p 4343 8.8.8.8
whois \-h 127.0.0.1 \-p 4343 AS3333
whois \-h 127.0.0.1 \-p 4343 \-\- '\-m 193.0.0.0/16'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect the live status without disturbing the server:
.PP
.RS
.nf
whoisd \-\-stats
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bind to the privileged port 43 without running as root:
.PP
.RS
.nf
setcap cap_net_bind_service=+ep /usr/local/bin/whoisd
.fi
.RE

.SH NOTES
.PP
Memory footprint scales with the size of the merged store. With all five
RIRs loaded, expect roughly 18\(en20 GiB resident. Startup time is dominated
by the initial download and parse; subsequent starts reuse the on-disk
cache and are bound by parse time alone.
.PP
.B whoisd
is a single static binary with no runtime dependencies beyond the host
operating system. It does not require a database server, message broker,
or external state store.

